      "documentSlug": "alpha-synuclein-biology-and-prion-like-spread",
      "documentConcept": "Alpha-synuclein biology and prion-like spread",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:77602556da3053e3ee52e2aa252f748ae6951119231845eb58b0add169f49281",
      "text": "Alpha-synuclein is concentrated at presynaptic terminals. Misfolded assemblies can template endogenous protein, and synthetic fibril inoculation produces spreading pathology in experimental mice. These findings motivate a prion-like model of progression. The reviewed literature distinguishes experimental propagation from human-to-human transmission, and published perspectives disagree about how fully spread explains human pathology. Aggregate conformation, cellular environment and the relative toxicity of oligomers and fibrils remain important questions. Braak staging proposed an ascending pattern of Lewy pathology, and a body-first versus brain-first hypothesis proposes different sites of origin.",
